@@s114 Greedy
@@s115 (a) Problem. (b) Sort the items in increasing order of key. Scan them in this order and add an item if feasible; otherwise skip it. (c) O(n log n) sort + O(n) scan.
@@s116 Gecko, counterexample
@@s117 Instance: small, no ties. Gecko's algorithm first picks … because …, then … and reaches value V. An optimal solution is … with value V' > V.
@@s118 Gecko, P ≠ NP
@@s119 Gecko's algorithm runs in O(…) time, which is polynomial. The problem is NP-complete [KT, Ch. 8]. If the algorithm were correct, an NP-complete problem would be in P, so P = NP.
@@s120 Graph traversal
@@s121 (b) The graph has one vertex per object, so n = …. There is an arc between u and v iff …, so m ≤ …. (c) I run BFS / Dijkstra [KT, Sec. …] from the start vertex and output …. (d) O(n + m).
@@s122 Dynamic programming
@@s123 (b) Let OPT(i, w) denote … using only items 1..i with budget w, for i ∈ {0..n} and w ∈ {0..W}. Base case: OPT(0, w) = …. For i ≥ 1: OPT(i, w) = max{skip, take}. The answer is OPT(n, W). (c) (n+1)(W+1) subproblems, O(1) each.
@@s124 Network flow
@@s125 (b) Given an instance, we construct a flow network: a source s, a sink t, one node per A and per B. Arcs s → a with capacity …; a → b with capacity 1 iff …; b → t with capacity …. By the integrality theorem there is an integral maximum flow. (c) Ford–Fulkerson [KT, Sec. 7.1] O(m'·|f*|).
@@s126 NP-hard
@@s127 (a) P1 = …. (b) P2 = …. (c) P2 ≤p P1. (d) Given an instance of P2, we construct an instance of P1 as follows: …. This takes polynomial time. If the P2 instance is a yes-instance, then …. Conversely, …. Example.
@@s128 NP certificate
@@s129 (a) Decision version: given …, is there …? (b) Certificate: the solution itself; its length is O(…). (c) The verifier checks every constraint in O(…) time.
@@s130 Divide and conquer
@@s131 If n ≤ 1, base case. Otherwise split into a parts of size n/b, recurse, and combine in O(n^c). T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(n^c), so T(n) = … by the master theorem.
@@s132 A.4 Facts to cite
@@s133 Sum of the degrees = 2m (handshake lemma). Tree: connected and acyclic ⇔ connected with n − 1 arcs.
@@s134 An undirected graph with m ≥ n edges has a cycle. Bipartite ⇔ no odd cycle.
@@s135 DAG ⇔ there is a topological order ⇔ the DFS finds no back edge.
@@s136 Cut property: the cheapest edge crossing a cut is in some MST (distinct weights). Cycle property: the most expensive edge of a cycle is in no MST.
@@s137 Dijkstra is correct only with weights ≥ 0. A reachable negative cycle makes the shortest paths undefined.
@@s138 S is a vertex cover ⇔ V \ S is an independent set: max IS + min VC = n. Clique in G = independent set in the complement.
@@s139 Weak duality: val(f) ≤ cap(A, B) for every flow f and every cut. Max-flow min-cut: value of the maximum flow = capacity of the minimum cut.
@@s140 Augmenting path theorem: f is maximum ⇔ there is no s → t path in the residual network. Integrality: integer capacities ⇒ integer maximum flow.
@@s141 Menger: maximum number of arc-disjoint s–t paths = minimum number of arcs to remove to separate s from t.
@@s142 Hall: a bipartite graph with |L| ≤ |R| has a matching covering L ⇔ |N(S)| ≥ |S| for every S ⊆ L. Kőnig: in a bipartite graph, minimum vertex cover = maximum matching.
@@s143 P ⊆ NP. NP-complete = in NP and NP-hard. If an NP-complete problem is in P, then P = NP. Transitivity: X ≤p Y and Y ≤p Z ⇒ X ≤p Z.
@@s144 Pseudo-polynomial: O(nW) is polynomial in W, not in log W. That is why knapsack has a DP and is still NP-complete.
