@@s229 A.7 Useful phrases (in English)
@@s230 <i>"in increasing / non-decreasing order of key"</i>; <i>"ties are broken arbitrarily"</i>.
@@s231 <i>"if and only if"</i>; <i>"without loss of generality"</i>; <i>"by induction on i"</i>.
@@s232 <i>"Given an instance of X, we construct an instance of Y as follows."</i>; <i>"a yes-instance of X is mapped to a yes-instance of Y"</i>.
@@s233 <i>"by the integrality theorem"</i>; <i>"by the max-flow min-cut theorem"</i>; <i>"an augmenting path in the residual network"</i>.
@@s234 <i>"the recurrence solves to"</i>; <i>"polynomial in the input size"</i>; <i>"pseudo-polynomial in W"</i>.
@@s235 A.8 Times and checklist
@@s236 4 hours = 240 minutes for 26–28 points. Initial reading and final re-reading about 15 minutes each. If a question gets stuck, write what you have and move on to the next.
@@s237 I wrote which problem.
@@s238 <b>Greedy:</b> explicit order with direction, admissibility test, time.
@@s239 <b>Graph:</b> nodes, arcs, how many, directed, weights; algorithm with name and starting node; what I print.
@@s240 <b>DP:</b> meaning of OPT, range, base cases, −∞ for the impossible, which cell is the answer, time and space.
@@s241 <b>Flow:</b> network for Sample Input 1, capacities, meaning of the max flow, algorithm and time.
@@s242 <b>NP-hard:</b> P2 ≤p P1 in the right direction, "Given an instance…", both directions, small example.
@@s243 Every time is in the parameters of the text.
